            <div class="legal-hero">
                <span class="legal-kicker">Privacy</span>
                <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
                <p class="legal-lead">BillWatch handles sensitive financial and account information. This policy explains what we collect, why we use it, how it may be shared, and the controls available to you.</p>
                <div class="legal-meta">
                    <span>Effective September 4, 2026</span>
                    <span>Last updated September 4, 2026</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="legal-callout">
                <strong>Core principle:</strong>
                BillWatch uses financial data to detect recurring bills, understand changes, and explain evidence. BillWatch is not designed to sell personal financial data or use connected bank data to move money.
            </div>

            <section id="scope">
                <h2>1. Scope</h2>
                <p>This Privacy Policy applies to BillWatch websites, web applications, APIs, account features, connected-financial-account features, statement-processing features, and related services that link to this policy.</p>
                <p>Third-party services you choose to use with BillWatch, including financial institutions and financial-data providers, may have separate privacy policies that govern their own collection and use of information.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="collect">
                <h2>2. Information BillWatch may collect</h2>
                <p>Depending on the features you use, BillWatch may collect or store:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Account information such as email address, display name, authentication state, security settings, and account preferences.</li>
                    <li>Connected financial institution and account metadata, including institution name, account type, account name, masks or partial identifiers, connection status, and synchronization metadata.</li>
                    <li>Transaction information authorized through a connected financial-data provider, such as transaction description, merchant, amount, date, category, and recurrence-related history.</li>
                    <li>Recurring Bill Streams, bill history, detected changes, alerts, confidence or validation state, and related derived records.</li>
                    <li>Documents you upload, including statements and images, together with extracted text, OCR output, structured bill fields, line items, validation results, and document-processing status.</li>
                    <li>Technical and security information necessary to operate the service, such as request metadata, authentication events, service health information, and limited diagnostic logs.</li>
                    <li>Communications you send to BillWatch and transactional email delivery information.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="financial">
                <h2>3. Connected financial data and Plaid</h2>
                <p>BillWatch may use Plaid to connect supported financial institutions. When you use that connection flow, Plaid may collect information directly from you under Plaid's own privacy practices and the permissions you grant.</p>
                <p>BillWatch receives only the financial information and connection data needed for the features you authorize, such as account metadata and transaction history. Server-side credentials or access tokens used to maintain a financial connection are treated as protected service credentials and are not intentionally exposed to browser JavaScript or the BillWatch client.</p>
                <p>BillWatch uses connected transaction history to discover recurring bills, monitor bill patterns, detect changes, and support related alerts and explanations. BillWatch does not use connected data to initiate payments, transfers, trades, or other movement of funds.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="statements">
                <h2>4. Statement and document data</h2>
                <p>If you upload a statement or supported document, BillWatch may store the file and process it to classify the document, extract text, run OCR when needed, parse structured bill information, validate candidate values, match the document to a Bill Stream, compare historical records, and detect changes.</p>
                <p>Statement files and derived records are intended to be scoped to the account that owns them. BillWatch does not intentionally return internal physical statement-storage paths to clients.</p>
                <p>Documents can contain information that is not needed for bill analysis. BillWatch's architecture is designed to minimize unnecessary exposure when data is passed to narrower processing components, including AI-assisted components where enabled.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="use">
                <h2>5. How BillWatch uses information</h2>
                <p>BillWatch may use information to:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Create and secure your account.</li>
                    <li>Connect and synchronize authorized financial accounts.</li>
                    <li>Discover recurring bills and build Bill Streams.</li>
                    <li>Process statements and other evidence you provide.</li>
                    <li>Compare bill history, detect meaningful increases or decreases, and calculate monthly or annualized impact.</li>
                    <li>Generate evidence-backed explanations and alerts.</li>
                    <li>Provide exports, account deletion, bank-disconnection, security, and preference features.</li>
                    <li>Send transactional account, security, verification, password-reset, and service communications.</li>
                    <li>Prevent abuse, investigate service failures, secure the platform, and maintain reliability.</li>
                    <li>Evaluate and improve bill-detection, statement-processing, and explanation quality.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="ai">
                <h2>6. AI-assisted and automated processing</h2>
                <p>BillWatch relies on deterministic software for core financial arithmetic, ownership and authorization checks, historical comparisons, and other verifiable system decisions. Where enabled, BillWatch may also use AI-assisted processing for narrowly scoped evidence-understanding tasks, such as identifying a candidate reason a statement changed.</p>
                <p>BillWatch's intended AI-processing design minimizes the information sent for a specific task and avoids intentionally sending credentials, access tokens, database connection strings, or unrestricted account history to an AI provider.</p>
                <p>AI output may be treated as a candidate fact rather than proof. BillWatch may validate or reject that output using deterministic rules and available evidence. If evidence is insufficient, BillWatch may report that the reason for a change is unknown.</p>
                <p>If BillWatch materially changes how AI providers are used with user financial evidence, this policy may be updated to reflect that change.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="sharing">
                <h2>7. When information may be shared</h2>
                <p>BillWatch may share information only as reasonably necessary to operate, secure, support, or legally provide the service. Categories of recipients may include:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Financial-data providers such as Plaid, when you use financial-account connection features.</li>
                    <li>Hosting, infrastructure, storage, backup, monitoring, and security service providers.</li>
                    <li>Transactional email providers used for verification, security, and account-recovery messages.</li>
                    <li>Document-processing or AI service providers where a specific feature is enabled and the provider is needed for that task.</li>
                    <li>Professional advisers, auditors, insurers, or legal authorities when reasonably necessary to protect BillWatch, comply with law, establish or defend legal rights, or respond to valid legal process.</li>
                    <li>A successor or transaction counterparty in connection with a merger, acquisition, financing, reorganization, sale of assets, or similar business transaction, subject to applicable law.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>BillWatch does not sell personal financial data for money and does not use connected bank transaction data for third-party behavioral advertising.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="retention">
                <h2>8. Data retention and deletion</h2>
                <p>BillWatch retains information for as long as reasonably necessary to provide and secure the service, maintain account and bill history, satisfy operational requirements, resolve disputes, enforce agreements, and comply with law.</p>
                <p>When you use BillWatch's account-deletion controls, the service attempts to revoke active bank connections before deleting associated user-owned records and stored statement files. If a required revocation or secure deletion step fails, BillWatch may stop the deletion workflow rather than report a deletion that did not safely complete.</p>
                <p>Deleted data may remain temporarily in encrypted or protected operational backups until those backups expire, are rotated, or are replaced through the normal backup lifecycle. BillWatch does not promise a specific backup-retention period in this policy unless one is separately published and operationally enforced.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="security">
                <h2>9. Security</h2>
                <p>BillWatch uses technical and organizational measures designed for sensitive financial information, including authenticated access, user ownership checks, server-side handling of protected financial connection credentials, anti-forgery protections for browser mutations, and protected storage and transport mechanisms appropriate to the deployed service.</p>
                <p>No internet-connected system can be guaranteed completely secure. You are responsible for using a strong password, protecting your device and recovery codes, enabling available two-factor authentication, and notifying BillWatch if you believe your account has been compromised.</p>
                <p>BillWatch does not claim a security certification or audit status unless that certification or audit has actually been completed and is expressly identified by BillWatch.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="choices">
                <h2>10. Your privacy choices and controls</h2>
                <p>Depending on available product features, you may be able to:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Disconnect a connected financial institution.</li>
                    <li>Review synchronized account and transaction information.</li>
                    <li>Export BillWatch data associated with your account.</li>
                    <li>Delete your BillWatch account and associated user-owned records through the account privacy controls.</li>
                    <li>Change your email address, password, account profile, and security settings.</li>
                    <li>Manage transactional or optional communications as supported by the service and applicable law.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Some information may need to be retained when required by law, necessary to resolve security or fraud issues, or present in ordinary backup retention as described above.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2>11. Data exports</h2>
                <p>BillWatch may provide a downloadable export of data associated with your account. The export is intended to include useful user-owned financial and BillWatch records while excluding protected connection credentials and internal statement-storage paths.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2>12. Email and communications</h2>
                <p>BillWatch may use your email address for sign-in, verification, password recovery, security notices, important service notices, and other transactional communications. Delivery providers may process the minimum email-address and message information necessary to deliver those communications.</p>
                <p>If marketing communications are introduced, they will be distinguished from essential transactional communications and managed in accordance with applicable law.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2>13. Cookies and local browser storage</h2>
                <p>BillWatch may use cookies or similar browser storage necessary for authentication, security, antiforgery protection, theme or presentation preferences, and reliable operation. BillWatch does not intentionally place Plaid access tokens or API refresh/access tokens into browser-readable storage.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="children">
                <h2>14. Children</h2>
                <p>BillWatch is not directed to children under 13 and is intended for people who can lawfully connect and manage their own financial accounts. BillWatch does not knowingly seek to collect financial information from children under 13.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2>15. United States operation and international users</h2>
                <p>BillWatch is currently developed and operated primarily for users in the United States. If you access BillWatch from another jurisdiction, your information may be processed in locations where BillWatch or its service providers operate, subject to applicable law.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="changes">
                <h2>16. Changes to this Privacy Policy</h2>
                <p>BillWatch may update this Privacy Policy as the product, service providers, data practices, or legal requirements change. Material changes may be communicated through the service, by email, or by another reasonable method. The effective date at the top identifies the current version.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="contact">
                <h2>17. Contact</h2>
                <p>Privacy questions, requests, or concerns may be submitted through the contact or support method published by BillWatch in the service. BillWatch may add a dedicated privacy contact address as the service moves beyond beta.</p>
            </section>

            <div class="legal-endnote">
                <strong>Beta legal draft.</strong>
                This policy is written to reflect BillWatch's current architecture and product behavior. It should be reviewed by qualified privacy counsel before broad commercial launch or expansion into jurisdictions with additional privacy requirements.
            </div>
